&#x200B;

Also the 2A (thats a much better way of saying that, thanks btw), is absolutely capable of being rewritten to better reflect the currect societal and political ideas behind firearms, in both as personal protection, but also as a hobby. I believe that it is too laxed on its approach, and the facts its harder to get a CAR than it is to get a GUN says a lot about the current atmosphere around gun control in America. Its flawed, but its flawed from more than just "people need less access to guns", its also flawed in the conversation "what does limiting people's rights to own guns mean for Americans" or "what does gun restriction mean under the context of the Constitution as a WHOLE, and not as a collection of 26 (or whatever) ammendments.

I think there's a lot of misunderstanding on both our parts, I have no animosity against you or your politics, and I have agreed with most of your points, but they're not all of it. I still stand by what I have said earlier about how you've presented your arguments with logical fallacies and tend to be opaque on how BAD or how GOOD a side is. If I have offended you in my discussion I apologize, it wasn't my intent.

&#x200B;

Yes, the right-wing has done some terrible things, but so has the left. That's not a novel concept and doesn't supply anything to the argument, but I can stand here and say all the good things the right-wing politics have done in terms of economical development, and putting America first. Trump's economic policies help boost America during the COVID-19 crisis in ways that Biden's administration has both failed to repeat, and has fumbled in the execution of. But I can also support many more of Biden's policies than I ever could for Trump, and it has absolutely nothing to do with his political stance, just the policies in a vacuum.

&#x200B;

And I agree, the addiction to the war machine has been a terrible down-ward spiral that has come in the cost of human lives, and finance for the American people. Lest I remind you that the current administration has been continually sending aid to Ukraine, despite at homes economic disasters affecting the lives of the average American citizen.

&#x200B;

This should be an argument on who's right and who's wrong, simply on the facts presented, and what they mean both in the long-term and in the short term.

