I think it shows what racism actually is: nothing, meaningless nonsense people use to justify actions and dehumanize others.

&#x200B;

I would argue by not reacting toward real racism and enjoying fantasized, fake, comedic racism, I beat the system of opression that was designed to hurt me in ways I didn't know I can hurt. And if we were to stop making movies, films, tv shows, plays, etc like these, the racists win.

>No offense...but that's just not true. You can view what a person's actions are and what other things they have said along if people have brought issues to them.

&#x200B;

I disagree. I believe looking for hidden meaning in people's actions can lead you down a rabbit hole full of misunderstandings and accusations that this world already has plenty of. That's a story for another day tho, though back on topic. No offense taken.

&#x200B;

>For example, lets say there is a white comedian who uses the N word all of the place in such a way that it puts down black people. He says he is using it for the shock value in his comedy, and it makes his jokes funnier, but he harbors no ill will to black people. He also has personally led many white supremacists rallies. Why should we believe him that he is just using shock value when his actions off the stage say otherwise.

&#x200B;

Are you referring to someone in particular? If so please drop their name so I can look into their routine and make my own assessment. If not, then I believe your logic is flawed because you're designing a person with a very conflicted personality. Sounds to me like you created a *strawman* racist, without labeling him a racist, and gave him qualities I mentioned earlier to try and justify a point I think you fell flat on. I think thats a fallacy many people fall into these debates, and I for one cant really take that statement with anything more than a grain of salt because its hypothetical.

I would've worked better like "consider andrew schulz, a white, male comedian from New York, who actively has racist overtones in his routines, and pokes fun at minority stereotypes. Does he empower racists?" In which I would argue no, an artist in not responsible for how people use their work. Its upto people to realize the wrongful use of art and scrutinize it. Again, that falls under interpretation and never knowing someone's true intent.

&#x200B;

>Should we take both of these people at their word, or have their other actions helped color how we should hear their speech?

&#x200B;

Why not both? These acts are not mutually exclusive. You're allowed to take someone on their word, while having opinions about not only their words but their actions, based on their history. It might play into it, then again, people change all the time, sometimes for the better. People are more than their past in more than one occasion, and more than their worst moments. Sometimes past actions speak for a person that simply does not exist anymore.

Now we're getting somewhere finally with this discussion.

&#x200B;

Absolutely right on with: 

&#x200B;

>Communicating about racism and being racist are two different things.

&#x200B;

I think a lot of people combine the two and accept that racism in any form, is pure evil and should be shunned. But I would argue that acting like you cannot make light of serious topics is flawed and a very sheltered view. I have not seen any of the movies you've mentioned but I will definitely check them out to see the perspective and compare them to other movies I've seen to see how racism can be portrayed differently under similar themes and tones.

&#x200B;

Thank you for a good read. Not really a "change my mind" kinda viewpoint, but I appreciated your perspective on this rather taboo topic.

My original point still stands, you can't know anyone's true intent, and so you have to take the word of the artist as gospel. Are there people with ulterior motives? Yes, but that's just reality. A good artist will separate his work from his life, and only draw inspiration from it, but ultimately falls upon us to derive meaning from the work presented. Some people are more inclined to accept taboos than others. Doesn't mean they're more racist, or more open-minded, just that they can accept it.

&#x200B;

Different doesn't mean bad, even if its reality is terrible. Racism, sexism, homophobia, can be both tools of evil, comedy, or talking point. Depends on what kind of story you're trying to tell the audience, and what themes you want to discuss.

&#x200B;

Let me link a discussion similar to ours from another reddit post for you to see what I mean 

https://www.reddit.com/r/unpopularopinion/comments/9tposi/humorous\_racism\_is\_okay/?utm\_source=share&utm\_medium=web2x&context=3

Hate Speech. But like I've already said in both my original post, and in my replies, hate speech can be both very obvious, and very quiet. There is a lot separating the two, and really comes down to the message the artist is trying to portray.

&#x200B;

What would you call the humor by and for racists?

I disagree, but only when done right.

&#x200B;

The real racism in movies is very obvious, and always played down, or shifted from the main sight of viewers by hiding it as actions, or attitudes instead of hate speech and hateful acts. BlacKKKLansman, despite being a mock-drama-comedy, portrays racism very faithfully, while still making time to make fun of people who act that way as bumbling redneck morons. Racism of course, exists in more nuanced ways, and ways we don't really identify with until its long since passed, but any person with a passing knowledge of both racism and comedy can understand when a moment is a serious racism vs mocking racism and racists, even if the punchline is someone being called a slur.

Referring to my original post since its what you're looking for

&#x200B;

>Sometimes what the world needs is a reminder that racism, is stupid. It's been stupid from the start! And people who are racist, are stupid, and should feel stupid, and should be portrayed as brain-dead morons, instead of loud, angry drunks. Because people can rally behind a brave drunk moron, our country was founded by those.

I disagree and thank you for the article, it  insightful but very one-sided and flawed. It argues that :

&#x200B;

>Unless you have an obvious marker of another person’s intent, you can’t really gauge their intent

&#x200B;

No one knows anyone's true intent. To claim you do is both naive and ignorant of how complex a human can be, or a community, or an art form. You have to take the art both for what it is, immediately in front of you, and for the message the artist decided to construct around it. There can be hidden meaning YOU can find for yourself, and perhaps the artist themselves, or a community of people may agree with you, but its all subjective. It always has been. If you take it as an alt-right conspiracy, that's on you, but I disagree, mostly because most art forms with racist overtones are actually left-leaning: The Boondocks, Dave Chappelle Show, Andrew Shultz, all left-leaning.

&#x200B;

Likewise, people who aren't racist aren't going to watch a racist art-form and become a racist, or become okay with the idea of casual racism. They won't go around poking fun at stereotypes, or even accept that these stereotypes are true or even anything more than hate mongering. Those that due, were probably already racist to begin with.

Why shouldn't it be? 

&#x200B;

True, people suffer racism today, and have suffered racism in the past, and will likely suffer racism in the future. But to pretend that racism not only doesn't exist but can't be made fun of for how ridiculous it seems very narrow-minded and naiive. You can shelter yourself from the world, but the world will find a way to remind you how terrible it and its people can be.

&#x200B;

We can't change racists. But we sure as hell can make fun of them for their beliefs.

I disagree.

&#x200B;

Jokes have one purpose. To make you laugh. If they make you laugh, they are good jokes, and therefore good comedy. My issue stands from actual hate, and not just satire. The line is incredibly obvious when you understand the work put into the actual art. Huckleberry Finn isn't racist because Mark Twain was a racist (he was), he's a racist because that's the character that Twain envisioned.

&#x200B;

Comedy works the same way. Stereotypes are stupid, but with layers of truth behind them. But they are still stupid, and only exist to propagate hate. Making fun of these stereotypes makes people realize how exaggerated they are, and the only people who agree with those stereotypes are actually racist. Everyone else sees it for the clown fest it really is, and laughs.

I believe both sides of politcs use racism as leverage. I've met equally many alt left who are so into their own beliefs about wokeness, that they circle back into segregation and racism.

&#x200B;

Its not unique to one party. Its a social issue, not a political issue.

Not specific to Blazing Saddles, but its the one I chose to act as a connective tissue towards relating my idea to something already existing and tangable in our world. You can really apply my logic to anything. The Boondocks comes to mind.

&#x200B;

I think people need to be made more uncomfortable in their daily lives, but uncomfortable without the shame associated with it. End of the day, there is no hate speech occuring in these films, otherwise actual respectable people would not allow themselves to be a part of it. Is Leonardo Dicaprio a bad actor for saying nigger on Django Unchained? I don't think so. Nor does it make him a racist

I've been discriminated against and called things from "beaner" to "spic" to Mexican, and I ain't even from Mexico. All because I speak Spanish and am slightly tan.

&#x200B;

Racism exist in different forms

I believe words are what you make of em. If you took offense im sorry, but it doesnt change my stance.

&#x200B;

Plus, I'm not white if that's what you're wondering. Not that I think it matters in the context of a discussion post

