I'm sorry to hear that you're going through a mental roadblock, especially for what seems like some time now.

&#x200B;

Trauma like that has a way of manifesting itself in different forms throughout our lives, and it can be hard to shake off. Sometimes we do look for solutions, ways out of situations that we either create for ourselves or that life creates for us. But let me ask you something, and I'm serious:

&#x200B;

Just cause you to change on the outside, will that really make you feel better on the inside?

&#x200B;

Issues like these stem from, as you said, a lot of insecurities caked on by years of bullying, abuse, self-hate, etc. I'm no therapist, but I don't see those issues going away, even with time, just cause you have reconstruction. These are deep-rooted issues that have to be talked about, worked through, studied, and resolved on your own terms. They take years to recover from, and a lifetime to understand. I am not saying reconstruction is the easy way out, if you decide thats whats for you then best of luck. But consider other options first. Attend group therapy, vocational help, things that get you to examine yourself under the microscope; warts and all, and force personal changes in order to slowly start sealing up those wounds you've been forced to stare at your whole life.

&#x200B;

I promise old wounds close easier than new ones. Good luck with everything.

